WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.800
Okay, I'm back.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.600
In this series of videos,

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.900
we're gonna cover customization of the shell,

4
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.400
multiple sessions support, scripts, race execs,

5
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.240
batch support, and translation issues.

6
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.760
A shell user has several options to personalize the shell.

7
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.480
A user can create their own .profile file

8
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.200
where user variables are defined to do things like

9
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.140
point to a log on script or define a search path

10
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.900
to be used when locating a file or a directory

11
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.420
and identify the editor to be used.

12
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.780
Environment variables can be defined in the login script.

13
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.980
The login script is started each time a shell is started.

14
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.900
The login script can contain all alias definitions

15
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.220
and other setup instructions.

16
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.820
By using the set command,

17
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.540
the user can set options for the shell session.

18
00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.940
These options control the way

19
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.700
the shell handles certain situations.

20
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.180
When the shell is invoked as a login shell,

21
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.540
.etc, .profile, and .home, .profile are used.

22
00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.540
If users want environment variables

23
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.700
in a shell invoked from the current shell,

24
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.440
they should identify a file in an env environment variable

25
00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.540
and place shell commands in the file.

26
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.460
The system interprets the file named in env

27
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.200
each time a new shell is invoked.

28
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.700
Depending on the commands used to set it,

29
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.880
an environment variable can be local,

30
00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.740
meaning it's used only for the current process

31
00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.620
or exported, used for the current process

32
00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:38.100
and for any other processes spawned by the current process.

33
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.900
There are many environment variables

34
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.340
set for your shell session,

35
00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.600
and if requested, they're available to all commands

36
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.400
or all programs that the shell runs as child processes.

37
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.860
Variables like log name, home, and shell

38
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.220
are set for you by RACF

39
00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.140
when you're validated as a user of the system.

40
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.920
Others like lock path, lib path, and man path

41
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.320
are lists of directories, search for locales,

42
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.880
dynamic load libraries, and online documentation pages.

43
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.080
The example shown here is from slash samples slash profile.

44
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.360
All shell users get the environment variables

45
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.360
set by this profile.

46
00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.360
Users can override some of the variables

47
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.880
by setting the variables in their private profiles

48
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.040
or in their shell sessions.

49
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.900
Note that all environment variables

50
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.140
are written with uppercase letters,

51
00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.300
and the contents of slash etc slash profile

52
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.220
are actually a shell script.

53
00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.360
It contains examples of the script programming languages

54
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.200
with if then statements.

55
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.620
It sets values for environment variables

56
00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.260
and it issues some shell commands

57
00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.320
like export, Umask, and read only.

58
00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.500
Any changes made in slash etc slash profile

59
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.740
will take effect the next time

60
00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.520
a user invokes the shell.

61
00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.160
Here we see three methods that shell users can use

62
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.520
to customize the environment variables for their shell.

63
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.940
They can all be used,

64
00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.360
but the last setting will take precedence.

65
00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.520
First up, the dollar home slash dot profile file.

66
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.140
A sample is provided in slash samples slash dot profile,

67
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.320
which can be copied

68
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.680
to a shell user's home directory and modified.

69
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.400
The env variable in the dollar home slash dot profile file

70
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:27.920
can be set to a file name,

71
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.800
which is a shell script that sets environment variables.

72
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.960
Shell users can use the env command

73
00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.920
to display the environment variables for their session

74
00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.680
and to change any of these variables.

75
00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.220
The change only lasts for the length of the session.

76
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:48.960
If you use the omvs command to log into the shell,

77
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.560
you can improve performance

78
00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.600
by using a log on procedure

79
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.600
that does not contain any job lib or step lib dd allocations.

80
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.920
This reduces the amount of storage that is copied for fork.

81
00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.120
It also prevents extensive searching of step live datasets

82
00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.760
and the propagation of step live datasets

83
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.200
from the shell process

84
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.360
to the shell command process on exec.

85
00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:17.560
Statements in either the slash etc slash profile

86
00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.200
or dollar home slash dot profile files as shown here,

87
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.120
improve the shell's performance for users

88
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.400
who enter the omvs command from ISPF

89
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.380
or with step live datasets allocated.

90
00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.840
We'll continue to look at the shell in the next video,

91
00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.320
starting with multi session support.
